    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cfd23fa5_907ad315",
        "filename": "include/uapi/linux/fuse.h",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 1015,
      "author": {
        "id": 1045980
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-05-14T00:22:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I can make ours use a __u64 type. I did notice bpf code does it both ways, so I\u0027m guessing newer ones use __u64, and the u32 one was just grandfathered in or something. The actual load set up by the respective convert_ctx_access does the expected width.\n\nSpecifically, struct __sk_buff uses a __u32 as a data pointer for the packet, which is then converted into a proper pointer sized load by a convert_ctx_access. Bpf programs then use some strange looking casts to make the compiler happy. It also feels really weird to call a number as a function, but that\u0027s just bpf I guess ;)",
      "parentUuid": "d0d22301_c050f3d6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1015,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1015,
        "endChar": 19
